course: everyday
lesson: 435
title: Boasting cousins and made-up holidays
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Cousin Jake from Texas came to stay and said he'd ridden a real bull at a rodeo «for like ten minutes». Grandpa laughed: «Son, the pros last eight seconds.» Everyone laughed, Jake too, and after that Grandpa asked «How many seconds?» every time Jake told a story. Knowing one real fact is often enough to spot a tall tale — and you can enjoy a tall tale without believing it.
explain: After holidays, stories grow. Priya said she'd been to the Maldives; asked about the water, she said «cold» — and her friend, who'd been, said «It's like a bath there.» Priya had stayed home and felt left out; when she admitted it, her friend said «Staying home is fine, you plank» (a friendly British way to say «you silly thing»), and they laughed. Harper's friend climbed the Eiffel Tower «on the stairs, all the way, in five minutes» — but the stairs only go to the second floor and the top is reached by lift. Most boasts are mostly true with one bit stretched; you don't have to call it out every time.
explain: Stories also grow in the telling. Isaac said «the pilot let me fly the plane». His friend's dad, a pilot, said kindly: «Kids can visit the cockpit sometimes, mate, but nobody flies it.» Isaac said «Okay, I sat in the seat when it was parked» — still cool, and he was glad he'd said it. Cousin Molly in Newcastle bragged about a pony, a laptop AND a trampoline; her mam rolled her eyes: «You got a jigsaw and socks, pet.» Molly sulked, then played the jigsaw with everyone, and it was canny — Geordie for nice.
explain: Too many tall tales cost you when you finally have a true one. Mila in Auckland really did swim with dolphins, but nobody believed her: «You said you met a mermaid last year.» She had to show a photo from her mum's phone. Sophie told a made-up ghost story at a sleepover as if it were true, and at three in the morning one girl was so scared she rang her mum to go home. Scary stories are fun when everyone knows they are stories.
explain: Theo in Melbourne said his pool was «as big as an Olympic one»; it was a paddling pool, and his mates jumped in anyway — «Sweet as» («great» in New Zealand and Australia). Callum's cousin said he was «bare close» (very close, London slang) with a famous YouTuber who texts him daily; the texts «disappear». He had once got a reply to a comment. Aiden's cousin said he had a girlfriend on holiday; his aunt said, «He's nine, Aiden. He means he waved at her.» Aiden kept it to himself, not wanting to embarrass him.
fact: Professional bull riders need to stay on for eight seconds; nobody rides a bucking bull for ten minutes.
fact: The sea around the Maldives is warm all year, about as warm as a bath.
fact: Visitors to the Eiffel Tower can climb stairs only to the second floor; the top is reached by lift, and the climb takes far more than five minutes.
fact: Children may sometimes visit a plane's cockpit, but only trained pilots fly the plane.
fact: Knowing a few real facts helps you spot exaggerations.
fact: Many boasts are mostly true with one bit stretched; stories often grow each time they are retold.
fact: Each made-up story spends some of your trust, so later even your true stories may not be believed.
fact: Making up a scary story as if it were true can frighten people badly; saying «this is made up» first keeps it fun.
fact: Not going away on holiday is normal, and true small stories make good conversations.
fact: Present contests make people lie, and nobody really wins them.
fact: You can quietly not believe a boast without embarrassing the person.
fact: «Canny» (North East England) means nice; «sweet as» (NZ, Australia) means great; «bare» (London) means very; «pet» (North East) is a kind name for someone.
exercise: How long did Jake say he rode the bull? | About ten minutes | About eight seconds | About one second | About an hour
exercise: How long do professional bull riders need to stay on? | Eight seconds | Eight minutes | One hour | Ten minutes
exercise: What gave away Priya's Maldives story? | She said the water was cold, but it's warm like a bath | She came back to school without even a little suntan | She had no photos at all | She said the Maldives is somewhere in the middle of Europe
exercise: Why did Priya make up the holiday? | She stayed home and felt left out | She really went but forgot most of it | Her teacher asked her to make one up | She wanted to win the class story prize
exercise: What did Molly really get for Christmas? | A jigsaw and socks | A pony and a new laptop | A big trampoline | Nothing at all
exercise: What did Isaac really do on the plane? | Sat in the pilot's seat while it was parked | Flew it for a short while over the clouds | Landed it with the pilot | Steered it over the sea with the pilot's help
exercise: Why didn't Mila's friends believe her dolphin story? | She had told so many fake stories before | Dolphins don't live anywhere near New Zealand | She had no swimming costume | Nobody in her class likes dolphins
exercise: How did Mila prove the dolphin story? | A photo on her mum's phone | A dolphin tooth she found on the beach | A video on her own phone | A postcard from the hotel
exercise: What did Callum's cousin say about the YouTuber's texts? | They disappear, it's a setting | They're too secret to show anyone, even family | They're all on his dad's phone | He deleted them all by mistake
exercise: What had the cousin really got from the YouTuber? | One reply to a comment, once | A long text every single day | A video call | A signed shirt
exercise: What happened after Sophie's ghost story at the sleepover? | One girl was too scared and called her mum at 3 am | Everyone fell asleep happily | They all went ghost hunting in the garden with torches until dawn | Sophie saw the ghost again
exercise: What was Theo's «Olympic» pool really? | A paddling pool | An indoor pool | A pond | A bath
exercise: How did Theo's mates react to the paddling pool? | Jumped in anyway and had a blast | Went home angry and didn't come back | Laughed at Theo all day | Asked to see the real pool
exercise: Why was Harper's friend's Eiffel Tower story stretched? | The stairs only go partway, and it takes much longer | The Eiffel Tower has no stairs at all, only lifts | Children can't visit the tower | It's in London, not Paris, so she couldn't have gone
exercise: What did Aiden's aunt say the cousin's «girlfriend» really meant? | He waved at a girl | He married a girl in secret | He met a famous girl | He had a pen pal in Spain
exercise: What did Aiden do with that information? | Kept it to himself to spare his cousin | Told all the boys at once | Teased his cousin about it all day long | Put it on the group chat
test: Grandpa: «How many seconds, son?» Why does he keep asking this? | To remind Jake how tall his tales are | Because he likes counting things out loud | Because Jake rides bulls every day on Grandpa's farm | Because rodeos have big timers on the wall
test: Molly's mam: «You got a jigsaw and socks, pet.» What does «pet» mean here? | A kind name, like «love» | A dog or cat | A rude word | A present
test: Mate: «Staying home is fine, you plank». Is she being mean? | No, it's a friendly joke and she means it kindly | Yes, she's very angry and wants a big fight after school | She thinks Priya is really made of wood, like a plank | She wants Priya to leave her alone and go home for good
test: What would have happened if Sophie had said «this is totally made up!» before the ghost story? | It could still be fun and nobody would be truly scared | Nobody would want to listen to a made-up story | Everyone would be more scared | The ghost would appear
test: Mila: «I swear I swam with dolphins!!» Friends: «you said you met a mermaid last year». What's the lesson? | Every lie spends trust you need later | Mermaids are real after all | Dolphins are fake | Never tell anyone any holiday stories at all
test: Isaac: «Okay, I sat in the seat when it was parked.» Why was he glad he said it? | The true story was still cool and he felt better | He got to fly the plane next time | The pilot gave him a medal | His friend's dad was angry
test: Theo's mate jumps in the paddling pool: «sweet as!» What does that mean? | Great, I love it | Too sweet | Too small | It's cold
test: Cousin: «we're bare close, he texts me every day». «Bare» means? | Very | Not at all | Naked | Sometimes
test: Your friend's holiday story is mostly true with one wild bit. What's the friendly approach? | Enjoy it — you don't have to call out every stretch | Shout «liar!» in front of everyone | Check everything online at once, while they talk | Never talk to them again
test: Molly sulked about her presents, then played the jigsaw. It was canny. How was it? | Nice | Boring | Scary | Too hard
test: In a film a boy really flies a passenger plane on holiday. What is true in real life? | Only trained pilots fly; kids may visit the cockpit | Children often fly planes if their parents agree | Anyone can fly if the pilot agrees | Planes fly themselves with kids
exam: Identify the principal means by which an exaggerated account of a rodeo ride may be detected. | Knowledge that professional riders are required to remain mounted for eight seconds | Knowledge that bulls are not found in Texas | Knowledge that rodeos are held only at night | Knowledge that children are not permitted to attend rodeos in any part of the United States
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child who, having frequently invented stories, subsequently relates a true one. | The true account is liable to be disbelieved | The true account is believed more readily | Previous inventions enhance the credibility of the child | The true account becomes false
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the ascent of the Eiffel Tower by visitors? | The stairs extend only to the second floor, the summit being reached by lift | The summit may be reached by stairs within five minutes | No stairs are available to visitors | Children are prohibited from ascending
exam: It is given that a child related a fictitious ghost story as true at a sleepover, whereupon another child requested to be collected at three o'clock in the morning. Determine the conduct most appropriate. | To state in advance that the story is invented | To insist thereafter that the story was true | To relate stories of a more frightening character on later occasions | To refrain from apologising
